var tafsirData_79 = {
  "1": "The sûrah opens with a series of oaths by categories of angels, beginning with “those who extract forcefully.” The word nāziʿāt refers to the angels who wrench out the souls of the disbelievers with harshness and severity, drawing them out from the depths of the body as a thorn is pulled from wet wool. This vivid image confronts the listener immediately with the reality of death and the power of the beings who carry it out, framing everything that follows within the certainty of the Hereafter.\n\nThe forceful extraction of the disbeliever's soul reflects the state of that soul at the moment of death. The disbeliever's soul is attached to the world, clinging desperately to the body and the life it has known, unwilling to be separated from them. It must therefore be torn away with violence, while the soul of the believer flows out gently, as will be described in the following verse. The manner of death is thus a reflection of the manner of life, and the extraction of the soul reveals what a person truly loved and clung to.\n\nThe oath by these angels is a device of the Qur'an to draw attention to the certainty of what follows. By swearing by the angels who extract souls, Allah is declaring, in effect, that the very existence of death — which no one denies — is a testimony to the reality of the Hereafter. If the soul is extracted at death, then death is not the end, and the soul that is taken must be returned for judgment. The disbelievers who doubted the resurrection are thus answered by the very process of death they could not avoid.\n\nThis verse sets the tone for the entire sûrah, which moves from the reality of death to the reality of resurrection to the certainty of the Day of Judgment. The believer is invited to reflect upon the angels and their work, to recognize the power of the One who created them, and to prepare for the moment when their own soul will be extracted and returned to its Lord.",
  "2": "This verse describes the second category of angels sworn by in the opening oaths: “those who draw out gently.” These are the angels who extract the souls of the believers with ease, tenderness, and gentleness, drawing them forth as smoothly as water is drawn from a vessel or as a strand of hair is pulled from dough. The contrast with the previous verse is deliberate and profound, for the same act of death is experienced in radically different ways depending upon the state of the soul being taken.\n\nThe gentle drawing out of the believer's soul reflects the believer's own state at the moment of death. The believer's soul is not attached to this world but yearns for its Lord, and when the angels come to take it, it flows towards them willingly, as a drop of water flows towards the sea. The believer has spent a lifetime preparing for this moment, purifying the soul and detaching it from the world, so that when death arrives it is not a violent wrenching but a gentle release, a homecoming rather than a separation.\n\nThe contrast between the two extractions teaches a profound lesson about the nature of attachment. The soul that is attached to the world experiences death as pain and loss, while the soul that is attached to Allah experiences death as ease and relief. This is why the believer strives throughout life to loosen the grip of the world upon the heart and to strengthen its bond with Allah. The ease of one's death is, in a sense, the measure of the purity of one's life.\n\nThis verse is a source of comfort and motivation for the believer. It comforts those who fear the pangs of death, reminding them that for the one whose heart is with Allah, death is gentle. And it motivates the believer to live in a way that will make their own extraction gentle, keeping their heart attached to their Lord so that when the angels come for them, the soul will be drawn out with the tenderness promised to the faithful.",
  "3": "This verse describes the third category of angels: “those who glide swiftly.” The word sābiḥāt refers to those who swim or glide, and the commentators explain that these are the angels who move through the heavens with the speed and grace of a swimmer moving through water. They descend and ascend, carrying out the commands of their Lord, traversing the vast distances between the heavens and the earth with a swiftness that defies human comprehension.\n\nThe image of gliding conveys both speed and ease. The angels do not struggle or labor in their movement; they flow through their appointed courses with perfect smoothness, as though the very fabric of creation were their medium. This is a glimpse of the order and harmony that pervades the unseen realm, where the angels move in perfect obedience, each one knowing its place and its task. It is a world of effortless motion, driven by complete submission to the divine will.\n\nThe swiftness of the angels is also a reminder of the power of Allah, who created these beings and who commands them as He wills. The one who sends the angels gliding through the heavens with such speed is surely able to gather humanity for judgment with equal ease. The oaths by these angels thus serve the central argument of the sûrah: the resurrection is not difficult for Allah, for the One who moves the angels through the cosmos can just as easily move the dead back to life.\n\nThis verse invites the believer to contemplate the grandeur of the angelic realm and, through it, the grandeur of the Creator. The unseen world is not empty or static but filled with the ceaseless activity of the angels, who glide through the heavens in the service of Allah. This awareness deepens the believer's sense of the reality and majesty of the divine, and strengthens their certainty in the unseen realities that the Qur'an reveals.",
  "4": "This verse describes the fourth category of angels: “those who race ahead.” These are the angels who hasten and compete in carrying out the commands of Allah, outracing one another in obedience, each one eager to be the first to fulfill its appointed task. The image is one of joyful, willing competition in the service of the divine, where obedience is not a burden but a delight, and where each angel strives to outdo the others in devotion.\n\nThe eagerness of the angels to race ahead in obedience stands in stark contrast to the sluggishness of human beings in responding to their Lord's commands. The angels, who have no sin and no rebellious inclination, obey Allah instantly and completely, racing to do His will. Human beings, burdened by desire and heedlessness, are often slow, reluctant, and negligent in their obedience. The verse thus holds up the angels as a model of what willing submission looks like, and implicitly reproaches human slowness.\n\nThe racing of the angels also points to the urgency of the divine command. When Allah wills something, there is no delay and no hesitation; the command is carried out immediately, by beings who move with the speed of light and the eagerness of love. This urgency foreshadows the theme of the sûrah: the coming of the Day of Judgment will be swift and sudden, carried out by these same angels with the same racing speed, leaving no time for excuses or delays.\n\nThis verse is an invitation to the believer to cultivate the same eagerness in their own obedience. The believer is urged to race towards good deeds, to compete in righteousness, and to respond to the call of Allah without delay. For just as the angels race ahead in the service of their Lord, so should the believer hasten towards what pleases Allah, seizing every opportunity for good before it passes.",
  "5": "This verse completes the series of oaths with the fifth category of angels: “those who arrange affairs.” These are the angels who are entrusted with the management and ordering of the affairs of creation, each one appointed over a specific task in the vast administration of the universe. Some arrange the winds and the rains, some manage the records of deeds, some guard the servants, and some execute the divine decrees. Together, they constitute the unseen machinery by which Allah directs the cosmos.\n\nThe image of angels arranging affairs conveys the order, precision, and wisdom with which the universe is governed. Nothing happens by chance or accident; every event, from the falling of a leaf to the rise and fall of nations, is part of a meticulously ordered plan, executed by angels who carry out their tasks with perfect fidelity. The believer is thus assured that the world is not chaos but a cosmos, governed by a wise and purposeful Lord through His obedient servants.\n\nThe arrangement of affairs by the angels culminates in the arrangement of the affairs of the Hereafter. The same angels who manage the world are the ones who will sound the Trumpet, gather humanity, and execute the judgments of the Day of Resurrection. The sûrah's opening oaths thus build towards this climax: the angels who extract souls, who glide, who race, and who arrange affairs are all participants in the great unfolding that will bring this world to its end and usher in the next.\n\nThis verse completes a portrait of the angelic realm that is both majestic and reassuring. The believer is reminded that they are not alone in an indifferent universe but surrounded by the unseen servants of a caring Lord, who has arranged every detail of existence with wisdom and purpose. This awareness deepens the believer's trust in Allah and their certainty that the promise of the Hereafter will be fulfilled with the same precision and order that governs all of creation.",
  "6": "The sûrah now transitions from the oaths to the event for which they prepare the listener: “the Day when the quaking blast will convulse.” This refers to the first blowing of the Trumpet, the moment when the order of the universe will be shattered and all creation will be shaken to its foundations. The word rājifah, the quaking, conveys a violent, terrifying convulsion that grips the heavens and the earth, a shaking so severe that everything that seemed solid and permanent will be thrown into chaos.\n\nThe quaking blast is the beginning of the end of the world as humanity knows it. The mountains, which had stood as symbols of stability and permanence, will crumble. The heavens, which had seemed unshakeable, will be rent asunder. The earth, which had been the firm ground beneath every footstep, will heave and convulse. This is the moment when the illusion of permanence in this world is shattered, and the reality of the Hereafter breaks through with overwhelming force.\n\nThe certainty of this quaking blast is established by the oaths that preceded it. The angels who extract souls, who glide through the heavens, who race in obedience, and who arrange affairs all testify to the power of the One who will bring about this Day. The disbelievers who doubted the resurrection are thus confronted with the testimony of the very beings they cannot see, sworn to by the Lord who created them. The quaking blast is not a myth or a metaphor but a certainty, appointed in the divine decree.\n\nThis verse is a call to awaken from the sleep of heedlessness. The world that seems so solid and so permanent is, in reality, temporary and fragile, awaiting only the command of its Lord to be shaken to pieces. The believer lives with this awareness, not in morbid fear but in wise preparation, knowing that the quaking blast is coming and that the only true security lies in the mercy of Allah.",
  "7": "This verse describes the continuation of the cosmic upheaval: “followed by the second blast.” After the first blowing of the Trumpet, which will convulse and destroy all things, comes a second blast, and this one will bring about the resurrection — the rising of all creatures from their graves to stand before their Lord for judgment. The sûrah thus compresses the stages of the end of the world and the beginning of the Hereafter into two decisive, overwhelming events: the blast that destroys and the blast that restores.\n\nThe second blast follows the first, as one wave follows another, with no interval of relief. There is no time for reflection or reconsideration; the unfolding of the divine decree is swift and continuous. What the first blast tears down, the second blast raises up, and between the two, the entire order of existence is transformed. The world passes away, and the Hereafter is established, all in the space of two trumpet blasts, by the simple command of the One who says “Be,” and it is.\n\nThe mention of the second blast answers the disbelievers' mocking question about the resurrection, which is quoted later in the sûrah. They asked how they could possibly be restored to life after becoming decayed bones. The answer is that it will take nothing more than a single blast, followed by another, and they will be standing before their Lord. The ease of the resurrection for Allah is emphasized throughout the Qur'an, for the One who created humanity from nothing can surely recreate them, and His command is but a single word.\n\nThis verse reinforces the sûrah's central message: the Day of Judgment is certain, near, and easy for Allah to bring about. The believer is urged to live in the light of this certainty, preparing for the moment when the second blast sounds and they are raised to face their reckoning. There is no escape from that moment, but there is preparation for it, and the wise believer begins that preparation now.",
  "8": "This verse describes the state of human hearts on that Day: “Hearts on that Day will be trembling.” The word wājifah conveys a violent, rapid beating, the palpitation of extreme terror and dread. On the Day of Resurrection, when the blasts have sounded and humanity is raised from the graves, the hearts of all will be seized with fear, pounding within the chests at the overwhelming reality that now confronts them. The composure and confidence that people maintained in this world will vanish in an instant.\n\nThe trembling of the hearts is universal, encompassing both believers and disbelievers, though with a crucial difference. For the disbelievers, the fear is the terror of the guilty, the dread of those who know they will face punishment for a lifetime of denial. For the believers, the fear is tempered by hope, the awe of standing before their Lord mixed with the trust in His mercy. Yet even the believers will tremble at the majesty of the Day, for the reality of standing before Allah is more awesome than anything the human heart can fully anticipate.\n\nThe trembling hearts on that Day contrast sharply with the hardened, heedless hearts of this world. In this life, many hearts are cold, unmoved by the remembrance of Allah and indifferent to the warnings of the Qur'an. But on the Day of Resurrection, that coldness will be shattered, and every heart will be filled with the fear that it ignored. The verse is a warning to soften the heart now, to let the fear of Allah enter it while there is still time, rather than to experience that fear suddenly and irreversibly on the Day it is too late.\n\nThis verse is an invitation to live with a heart that is alive, sensitive, and responsive to the truth. The believer is urged not to let their heart become hardened by heedlessness, but to keep it soft through remembrance, reflection, and the fear of Allah. For the heart that trembles now, in awe of its Lord, will be among the hearts that are given peace on the Day when all other hearts tremble.",
  "9": "This verse completes the description of the state of the deniers on the Day of Resurrection: “Their eyes will be downcast.” Having described the trembling of the hearts, the Qur'an now describes the lowering of the eyes, the physical manifestation of shame, humiliation, and fear. The eyes that in this world were raised in arrogance, that looked upon the truth with disdain and upon the believers with contempt, will now be cast down in humiliation, unable to meet the reality that confronts them.\n\nThe downcast eyes are the opposite of the proud, defiant gaze of the disbeliever in this world. In this life, they walked with their heads held high, scorning the warnings of the prophets and mocking the promise of the Hereafter. Their eyes reflected the arrogance of their hearts. On the Day of Resurrection, that arrogance will be broken, and their eyes will fall, for there will be nothing left to be proud of. The humiliation that they denied in this world will be their reality in the next.\n\nThe lowering of the eyes is also a gesture of fear, the instinctive response of one who cannot bear to look upon what they see. The terror of the Day will be so overwhelming that the deniers will not be able to raise their eyes to face it. This is the fear that they dismissed in this world, when they were told to fear the Day of Judgment and they laughed it off. Now that fear has come upon them, and they can neither bear it nor escape it.\n\nThis verse is a reminder of the reversal that the Hereafter will bring. The proud will be humbled, the arrogant will be shamed, and the defiant will be subdued. The believer is urged to cultivate humility now, to lower their gaze in this world in submission to Allah, so that on the Day of Resurrection their eyes may be raised in honor rather than cast down in humiliation. For the honor of that Day belongs to the humble, and the shame belongs to the arrogant.",
  "10": "This verse records the mocking question of the disbelievers: “Will we really be returned to our former state?” This is the skepticism with which they greeted the message of the resurrection, the incredulity of those who could not conceive of being brought back to life after death. The word ḥāfirah refers to the original state, the condition of life before death, and the disbelievers ask, with mockery and disbelief, whether they will truly be restored to it. Their question is not a genuine inquiry but a scoffing dismissal.\n\nThe question reveals the fundamental limitation of their thinking. They measured the power of Allah by their own weakness, assuming that because they could not conceive of the resurrection, it could not happen. They forgot that the One who created them from nothing in the first place is fully capable of creating them again. Their skepticism was not the skepticism of reason but the skepticism of arrogance, the refusal to accept anything that challenged their comfortable denial of accountability.\n\nThe Qur'an quotes this question precisely in order to answer it, as the following verses do. The disbelievers' own words are turned against them, exposed as shallow and self-defeating. The very difficulty they found in the resurrection is answered by the ease with which Allah will accomplish it. Their mockery becomes the occasion for the affirmation of the truth, and their doubt becomes the springboard for certainty.\n\nThis verse is a timeless portrait of the human tendency to doubt what one does not wish to believe. The disbelievers doubted the resurrection not because the evidence was lacking but because accepting it would have required changing their lives. The believer, by contrast, accepts the resurrection because they trust the One who promised it, and that acceptance transforms the way they live. The question “will we really be returned?” is answered by a life lived in preparation for that return.",
  "11": "This verse continues the disbelievers' mocking question: “Even after we have become decayed bones?” Their skepticism focused on the physical impossibility, as they saw it, of resurrecting bodies that had decomposed and turned to dust. How could bones that had rotted away, scattered and disintegrated, possibly be reassembled into a living human being? This objection seemed conclusive to them, the ultimate refutation of the claim of resurrection. In their minds, the decay of the body was the final end, and nothing could reverse it.\n\nThe objection is answered throughout the Qur'an with the reminder that the resurrection is not difficult for Allah. The One who created the human being from a drop of fluid, who formed the bones and clothed them with flesh, who brings the dead earth back to life with rain, is surely able to reassemble decayed bones and restore them to life. The disbelievers' objection reveals only their failure to appreciate the power of their Creator, whom they saw at work every day in the world around them but refused to recognize.\n\nThe mention of decayed bones is significant because it confronts the reality of death in its most unappealing form. The disbelievers used the image of rotting bones to make the resurrection seem absurd, but the image actually serves the opposite purpose: it demonstrates the extent of Allah's power. The more complete the decay, the more miraculous the restoration. What is impossible for human power is effortless for the divine, and the decay of the body is no obstacle to the One who said “Be,” and it was.\n\nThis verse, with its vivid image of decayed bones, is a call to reflect upon the power of Allah rather than to mock it. The believer looks at the decay of death and sees the promise of resurrection, knowing that the Lord who created life from lifelessness can restore life to the dead. The disbeliever looks at the same decay and sees only the end. The difference is not in the evidence but in the willingness to see.",
  "12": "This verse records the disbelievers' sarcastic conclusion: “They say, 'That would then be a losing return!'” Having mocked the possibility of the resurrection, they now mock its consequences, declaring that if such a return were true, it would be a return of loss. The word kharrah, return, echoes their earlier question about returning to their former state, but now it is paired with khāsirah, losing — a return that brings nothing but loss and ruin. Their mockery, however, unwittingly speaks the truth, for their return will indeed be one of catastrophic loss.\n\nThe irony of their statement is profound and deliberate. They meant it as a sarcastic dismissal, as if to say, “If we are resurrected, then we will be the losers — and how absurd that is!” But the Qur'an quotes their words to reveal that they have, without realizing it, stated the truth. Their return will be a losing return indeed, for they will return to face the consequences of a life of denial and will find that they have lost everything of value. Their own words become a prophecy of their own ruin.\n\nThe concept of loss is central to the Qur'an's understanding of the Hereafter. The one who loses faith loses everything, for they have traded the eternal for the temporary and the real for the illusory. The disbeliever who mocks the resurrection is, in reality, the one who stands to lose the most. Their sarcasm is the bravado of one who has not yet faced the reality they mock, and when that reality arrives, their laughter will turn to lament.\n\nThis verse is a reminder that the mockery of the disbelievers does not diminish the truth but rather testifies to it. The believer is not shaken by the ridicule of the skeptics, for the believer knows that the mockers are, in their own words, the ones who will suffer the losing return. The believer's task is to live in a way that makes their return a winning one, a return to the mercy and pleasure of their Lord.",
  "13": "This verse delivers the decisive answer to the disbelievers' mockery: “Indeed, it will be but a single blast.” The resurrection, which they found so inconceivable, will be accomplished with the greatest of ease. It will require nothing more than a single cry, a single command, a single blast of the Trumpet, and the dead will rise. The word zajrah, blast, conveys the idea of a sharp, sudden cry or shout, a single sound that pierces the stillness of the graves and summons every soul back to life.\n\nThe emphasis on “a single blast” is the Qur'an's way of demolishing the disbelievers' objection. They imagined that the resurrection would require some impossible, laborious process. The Qur'an responds that it will be the opposite — a single, effortless command. This is the recurring theme of the Qur'an's argument for the resurrection: the One who created the universe with a word can recreate humanity with a word. The difficulty that so impressed the disbelievers is nothing before the power of Allah.\n\nThe brevity of the blast also conveys the suddenness of the event. There will be no warning, no gradual transition, no time to prepare. In a single instant, the order of this world will be overturned and the order of the Hereafter established. This suddenness is itself a warning, for it means that the opportunity for preparation is now, in this life, and not later. The one who delays their repentance, thinking they have time, is reminded that the blast may come without notice.\n\nThis verse is the heart of the sûrah's response to the skeptics, and its message is one of absolute certainty. The resurrection is not a matter of debate but of divine decree, and it will be accomplished with a single blast. The believer accepts this with the ease of faith, and lives in constant readiness for that blast, knowing that it will come, and that when it does, the time for action will have passed forever.",
  "14": "This verse completes the description of the resurrection: “And at once they will be above ground.” The word sāhirah refers to the surface of the earth, the open plain upon which humanity will be gathered after the resurrection. In a single instant, following the single blast, all of humanity will find themselves standing upon the earth, raised from their graves, gathered together for the judgment of their Lord. The transition from the stillness of death to the activity of the gathering is instantaneous and total.\n\nThe phrase “at once” captures the suddenness and totality of the event. There is no intermediate state, no gradual process of reawakening. One moment the dead are in their graves; the next, they are standing above ground, alive and aware. This is the power of the single blast, the command of Allah that nothing can resist. The graves, which had held their occupants so securely, are opened, and the dead pour forth as a locust swarm, summoned to the plain of gathering.\n\nThe image of standing above ground is one of exposure and accountability. The dead who had been hidden beneath the earth, their deeds concealed, are now brought out into the open, where nothing is hidden. The secrets of every soul are laid bare, and every person stands exposed before the judgment of Allah. This is the moment that the Qur'an repeatedly warns about, the moment when the veil of this world is lifted and the reality of the Hereafter is made manifest.\n\nThis verse brings the sûrah's account of the resurrection to its culmination, and its message is one of overwhelming certainty. The disbelievers who mocked the idea of returning to life are answered with the fact of it: a single blast, and they will be above ground, facing the consequences of their mockery. The believer receives this message not with fear alone but with hope, for the same blast that raises the disbelievers to judgment raises the believers to their reward.",
  "15": "The sûrah now shifts from the cosmic drama of the resurrection to a historical narrative, asking: “Has the story of Moses reached you?” This question, addressed to the Prophet, peace be upon him, introduces the account of Moses and Pharaoh as a lesson and a consolation. The story of Moses is not merely ancient history but a living parable, demonstrating the same truths that the sûrah has been affirming: the power of Allah, the certainty of His judgment, and the fate of the arrogant who defy Him.\n\nThe question is rhetorical, drawing attention to the significance of what follows. The Prophet was familiar with the story of Moses, but the Qur'an recounts it anew to extract its lessons for the present. The confrontation between Moses and Pharaoh is the archetype of the confrontation between truth and tyranny, between the humble servant of Allah and the arrogant denier of His power. It is a pattern that repeats in every age, and its outcome is always the same.\n\nThe story of Moses serves as a warning to the disbelievers of the Prophet's own time, who were repeating the arrogance of Pharaoh. Just as Pharaoh defied Moses and was destroyed, so too would the opponents of the Prophet face destruction if they persisted in their defiance. The story also consoles the Prophet, reassuring him that the trials he faced were not unique but part of the pattern of every prophet, and that the end of the defiant is always ruin.\n\nThis verse is an invitation to learn from history, to see in the stories of the past the lessons for the present. The believer is urged to reflect upon the story of Moses, to recognize the power of Allah that it displays, and to take warning from the fate of those who opposed His messengers. For the story of Moses is not merely a tale of long ago but a mirror in which every generation may see its own choices and their consequences.",
  "16": "This verse begins the account of Moses' call to prophethood: “When his Lord called him in the sacred valley of Tuwa.” The scene is set in the sacred valley of Ṭuwā, a place sanctified by the divine presence, where Moses, journeying with his family, encountered the burning bush and heard the voice of his Lord. This was the moment that transformed Moses from a fugitive shepherd into a prophet, the moment when Allah spoke to him directly and entrusted him with a mission of the greatest magnitude.\n\nThe description of the valley as “sacred” (al-muqaddas) emphasizes the honor and sanctity of the place where Allah chose to speak to His servant. The valley was not sacred in itself, but it became sacred through the event that occurred there, the meeting between the Creator and His chosen messenger. This teaches that places are sanctified by the presence of Allah's remembrance and the events of His revelation, not by any inherent quality of their own.\n\nThe direct call of Allah to Moses is a mark of the highest honor. Moses is among the few to whom Allah spoke directly, without the intermediation of an angel, and this direct address is the foundation of his prophetic authority. The verse invites reflection upon the intimacy of this moment — the Creator speaking to His creature, calling him by name, in the stillness of the sacred valley. It is a reminder of the closeness that Allah grants to those whom He chooses and whom He loves.\n\nThis verse sets the stage for the mission that follows, the confrontation with Pharaoh that forms the heart of the story. The reader is prepared to see how the humble servant, called in the sacred valley, will stand against the mightiest tyrant of his age, armed with nothing but the word of his Lord. It is the eternal drama of faith against power, and its outcome is never in doubt.",
  "17": "This verse records the first part of the command given to Moses: “Go to Pharaoh, for he has truly transgressed.” Moses, who had fled Egypt in fear for his life, is now commanded to return to the very heart of the tyrant's power and confront him directly. The mission is daunting in the extreme, for Pharaoh was the absolute ruler of Egypt, claiming divinity over his people, and Moses was a wanted fugitive. Yet the command is clear and unequivocal: go to Pharaoh and deliver the message.\n\nThe reason for the mission is stated plainly: Pharaoh had transgressed. The word ṭaghā conveys the idea of overflowing beyond all bounds, of arrogance so extreme that it acknowledges no limit and no authority above itself. Pharaoh's transgression was not merely political but spiritual, for he had claimed to be a god and had enslaved and oppressed the Children of Israel. His tyranny was the fullest expression of human arrogance, the refusal to bow before any power greater than oneself.\n\nThe command to go to Pharaoh, despite his power and his transgression, is a lesson in the nature of the prophetic mission. The prophet is sent to confront tyranny, not to accommodate it; to speak truth to power, not to flatter it. Moses was sent to the mightiest tyrant of his age with nothing but the word of Allah, and this word proved mightier than all of Pharaoh's armies. The believer learns that no earthly power is to be feared when one carries the message of the All-Powerful.\n\nThis verse is a reminder that the battle against transgression is central to the mission of faith. The believer is called to oppose arrogance and injustice wherever they are found, trusting in Allah rather than in worldly strength. For the one who goes to Pharaoh with the word of Allah goes with the greatest of allies, and the tyrant who seems invincible is, in reality, utterly defenseless before his Lord.",
  "18": "This verse records the second part of the command given to Moses: “And say, 'Would you be willing to purify yourself?'” The message that Moses was to deliver to Pharaoh, the tyrant who claimed divinity, begins not with a threat but with an invitation — an invitation to purification. This is a profound insight into the nature of the prophetic message. Even the worst of tyrants, the most arrogant of sinners, is offered the chance to purify himself and to return to the path of righteousness. The door of mercy is open to all, even to Pharaoh.\n\nThe concept of purification (tazakkā) is central to the Islamic understanding of spiritual growth. It refers to the cleansing of the soul from the impurities of sin, arrogance, and disbelief, and its cultivation in the virtues of faith, humility, and obedience. Moses was offering Pharaoh the opportunity to cleanse himself of the greatest of all impurities — the claim to divinity — and to return to the worship of the one true God. It was an offer of salvation, extended even to the worst of tyrants.\n\nThe gentleness of the invitation is striking. Moses did not begin with condemnation but with a question, an opening that respected Pharaoh's agency and invited his reflection. This teaches the manner in which the message of truth is to be conveyed: with gentleness, wisdom, and respect, even to those who seem most opposed to it. The prophet's role is to invite, not to compel, and the invitation is always offered before the warning.\n\nThis verse is a reminder of the infinite mercy of Allah, who offered purification even to Pharaoh, the man who claimed to be God. If the door of mercy was open to Pharaoh, it is open to every sinner, and no one should despair of Allah's forgiveness. At the same time, the verse reminds the believer of the proper manner of conveying the message: with gentleness and invitation, leaving the outcome to Allah.",
  "19": "This verse completes the invitation that Moses was to extend to Pharaoh: “And I will guide you to your Lord, so that you will fear Him.” The message is now made explicit. Moses offers to guide Pharaoh to the knowledge of his Lord, the one true God, in place of the false divinity that Pharaoh had claimed for himself. The goal of this guidance is described as the fear of Allah — not a servile terror, but the reverent awe and awareness of Allah that is the foundation of all righteousness and the beginning of all purification.\n\nThe invitation to be guided to one's Lord is the essence of the prophetic mission. Every prophet was sent to guide humanity to Allah, to lead them from the darkness of ignorance and disbelief into the light of knowledge and faith. Moses was offering Pharaoh the greatest gift that can be offered: the knowledge of his Creator and the fear of Him that brings salvation. The one who knows his Lord and fears Him is purified of arrogance, for how can a creature claim divinity when he stands in awe of his Creator?\n\nThe fear of Allah described here is the root of all virtue. It is the awareness that keeps a person from sin, the reverence that inspires obedience, and the awe that grounds humility. The one who fears Allah in this sense is protected from every evil, for the fear of Allah is the guardian of the heart. Pharaoh, who feared no one, was the most corrupt of men precisely because he had lost this fear. Moses offered to restore it to him, and with it, his humanity.\n\nThis verse, together with the previous one, reveals the full beauty of the invitation extended to Pharaoh. It was an invitation to purification, to guidance, and to the fear of Allah — in short, an invitation to salvation. That Pharaoh rejected it is the tragedy of the story, and his rejection sealed his fate. The believer is reminded to accept the same invitation, extended to all of humanity, and to live in the fear and knowledge of their Lord.",
  "20": "This verse describes the culminating act of Moses' confrontation with Pharaoh: “So he showed him the greatest sign.” After delivering the message, Moses demonstrated the truth of his claims with a sign of overwhelming power — according to the commentators, the staff that turned into a serpent, the greatest of the signs that Moses performed. This sign was not a trick or an illusion but a manifest demonstration of the power of Allah, a proof that Moses spoke on behalf of the One who controls all of creation.\n\nThe description of the sign as “the greatest” (al-kubrā) emphasizes its overwhelming nature. It was not a minor wonder but a sign of such magnitude that it should have left no doubt in any reasonable mind. The staff, a simple piece of wood, was transformed into a living serpent, defying every law of nature. This was the signature of the divine, the unmistakable mark of a power beyond all human capability. Pharaoh, witnessing it, was faced with the choice of submission or defiance.\n\nThe showing of the sign completes the pattern of the prophetic mission: invitation followed by evidence. Moses did not ask Pharaoh to believe without proof. He offered the message, then demonstrated its truth with signs that no one could produce except the One who sent him. This pattern is repeated throughout the Qur'an, for Allah has never left humanity without guidance or without evidence. The rejection of the truth is never the result of a lack of evidence but of a refusal to submit.\n\nThis verse sets the stage for the tragedy that follows, for despite the greatest sign, Pharaoh rejected and defied, as the next verses describe. The believer is reminded that signs alone do not produce faith; faith requires a heart willing to submit. The believer's task is to keep their own heart open to the signs of Allah, recognizing in them the proof of His power and the call to His worship, rather than hardening the heart as Pharaoh hardened his.",
  "21": "This verse records Pharaoh's response to the greatest sign: \"but he denied and disobeyed ˹Allah˺.\" Despite witnessing the staff transform into a serpent before his eyes, despite the overwhelming evidence of Moses' truthfulness, Pharaoh chose denial and disobedience. His response was not born of ignorance but of arrogance, for he understood the truth of Moses' message yet refused to submit to it, because submission would have required him to relinquish his claim to divinity and to humble himself before a Lord greater than himself. The verse captures the essence of his crime in two words: denial and disobedience.\n\nThe pairing of denial (kadhdhaba) and disobedience (ʿaṣā) is significant, for it describes the two dimensions of Pharaoh's rebellion. His denial was the rejection of the truth in his heart and on his tongue, while his disobedience was the refusal to act upon that truth in his life. Together they represent the complete rejection of the divine message — a rejection not of the intellect, which recognised the truth, but of the will, which refused to yield. This is the archetype of all rejection, repeated in every age by those who see the truth but choose their own desires and their own status over it.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer that denial is often not a matter of evidence but of submission. Pharaoh had all the evidence he could ask for, yet he denied because he would not bow. The believer is reminded that faith is not merely the recognition of truth but the submission of the will to it, and that the greatest barrier to faith is often the arrogance that refuses to surrender. The verse also serves as a warning, for Pharaoh's denial and disobedience led to his destruction, and the same fate awaits all who follow his example.",
  "22": "This verse describes the next stage of Pharaoh's rebellion: \"then turned his back, striving ˹against the truth˺.\" Having denied and disobeyed, Pharaoh did not simply remain passive in his rejection; he actively turned his back on the truth and devoted himself to opposing it. The word \"striving\" (saʿā) indicates earnest, determined effort, and it reveals that Pharaoh threw himself fully into the campaign against Moses and his message. His rejection was not a momentary lapse but a committed, active rebellion, pursued with all the energy and resources at his disposal.\n\nThe image of \"turning his back\" is significant, for it describes the deliberate turning away from the truth after it has been made clear. Pharaoh did not face the truth and wrestle with it; he turned his back on it, refusing even to look at it, and then busied himself with opposition to it. This is the pattern of the arrogant, who, when they cannot refute the truth, turn away from it and devote themselves to fighting it, hoping that by their efforts they can suppress what they cannot answer.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer about the nature of opposition to the truth. Those who reject the truth rarely do so passively; more often, they become active opponents, striving to silence it, to discredit it, and to prevent others from hearing it. The believer is reminded that this opposition is a sign of the truth's power, for falsehood does not need to be opposed with such energy; only the truth, which threatens the foundations of arrogance and falsehood, provokes such a response. The believer is encouraged to remain steadfast in the face of active opposition, trusting that the truth will ultimately prevail, as it did over Pharaoh.",
  "23": "This verse describes Pharaoh's next move: \"Then he summoned ˹his people˺ and called out.\" Having turned his back on the truth and resolved to oppose it, Pharaoh gathered his people — his ministers, his officials, and the masses — and addressed them in a grand public display. The word \"summoned\" (ḥashara) conveys the idea of gathering together, while \"called out\" (nādā) indicates a loud, public proclamation. Pharaoh was not content to reject Moses privately; he sought to rally his entire kingdom against him, turning his rejection into a public spectacle designed to intimidate and impress.\n\nThe public nature of Pharaoh's opposition is significant, for it reveals the tactics of tyranny. A tyrant who feels threatened by the truth does not respond with quiet reflection but with a public show of force, seeking to drown out the truth with noise and to overwhelm the solitary voice of the messenger with the collective weight of the masses. Pharaoh's summoning of his people was an act of political theatre, designed to demonstrate his power and to isolate Moses and his message.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer about the nature of the struggle between truth and falsehood. The truth often begins with a single voice, while falsehood commands the crowds and the institutions of power. Yet the outcome is never determined by numbers, for the truth of Moses, armed with the word of Allah, ultimately prevailed over the entire apparatus of Pharaoh's state. The believer is reminded that the power of the truth does not lie in numbers but in its divine source, and that the public shows of falsehood are no match for the quiet certainty of faith.",
  "24": "This verse records the climax of Pharaoh's arrogance: \"saying, 'I am your lord, the most high!'\" In his public address, Pharaoh declared himself to be the supreme lord of his people, claiming for himself the very attribute that belongs to Allah alone — the Most High. This was the fullest expression of his transgression, the moment when his arrogance reached its peak and he openly usurped the position of the Creator. The claim \"I am your lord, the most high\" is the ultimate act of rebellion, for it is not merely the rejection of a messenger but the denial of Allah Himself.\n\nThe claim of Pharaoh is the archetype of all human arrogance, the attempt of a created being to elevate itself to the position of its Creator. Every form of tyranny, every act of oppression, and every claim of absolute authority is a distant echo of this original rebellion. Pharaoh's declaration reveals what lies at the heart of all falsehood: the refusal to acknowledge any power above oneself, and the desire to make oneself the object of worship and obedience in place of Allah.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer about the gravity of arrogance and the reality of Pharaoh's crime. The claim to divinity is the greatest of all transgressions, and it is the sin for which Pharaoh is remembered as the archetype of tyranny. The believer is reminded that true greatness belongs to Allah alone, and that the one who claims it for themselves has set themselves against the Lord of the worlds. The verse also serves as a warning against the arrogance that exists, in lesser forms, in every human heart — the pride that seeks to elevate the self above others and above the authority of Allah.",
  "25": "This verse describes the fate of Pharaoh: \"So Allah overtook him, making him an example in this life and the next.\" Having declared himself the most high lord, Pharaoh was seized by the punishment of Allah, who overtook him with a punishment that encompassed both this world and the Hereafter. In this world, he was drowned in the sea along with his army, and in the Hereafter, he faces the eternal punishment of the Fire. His claim to divinity was answered by the One Whose power he had denied, and his arrogance was met with a humiliation that reversed his boast.\n\nThe word \"overtook\" (akhadha) conveys the sense of being seized and overtaken by an inescapable force, while \"making him an example\" (nakāl) refers to the exemplary punishment that serves as a warning to others. Pharaoh's fate was not merely his own destruction but a lesson for all of humanity, a sign that the claims of the arrogant will always be answered by the power of Allah. The phrase \"in this life and the next\" emphasises the completeness of his punishment, which was not deferred to the Hereafter but began in this world with the drowning and continues in the Hereafter with the Fire.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer about the certainty of divine justice. The tyrant who appears invincible in this world is, in reality, utterly vulnerable before the power of Allah, and his apparent success is only a prelude to his downfall. The believer is reminded that the arrogance of the powerful will always be answered, that the truth will always be vindicated, and that the fate of Pharaoh is a warning to all who follow in his path. The verse is a source of hope for the oppressed and a warning to the oppressor.",
  "26": "This verse draws the lesson from the story of Pharaoh: \"Surely in this is a lesson for whoever stands in awe of ˹Allah˺.\" The story of Pharaoh — his arrogance, his claim to divinity, and his destruction — is described as a lesson (ʿibrah) for those who fear Allah. The word ʿibrah refers to a lesson that is passed across, from one thing to another, conveying the idea that the fate of Pharaoh is a warning that crosses over from history into the present, instructing those who reflect upon it. The story is not merely an account of the past but a living lesson for the present and the future.\n\nThe verse specifies that this lesson is for \"whoever stands in awe of ˹Allah˺\" — the one who fears Allah and is conscious of His power. It is this fear of Allah that opens the heart to receive the lesson, for the one who stands in awe of their Lord will see in the fate of Pharaoh a warning for themselves, while the one whose heart is hardened will dismiss it as mere history. The fear of Allah is thus presented as the key that unlocks the wisdom of the Qur'an's stories, transforming them from narratives into guidance.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the purpose of the Qur'an's historical accounts. They are not told for entertainment but for instruction, and their lessons are available to all who approach them with the fear of Allah. The believer is encouraged to reflect upon the story of Pharaoh, to see in it the fate of arrogance, and to cultivate in their own heart the awe of Allah that makes such reflection fruitful. The story of Moses and Pharaoh thus becomes, for the believer, a personal warning and a call to humility and submission.",
  "27": "This verse transitions to a new argument for the resurrection: \"Which is harder to create: you or the sky? He built it.\" Having recounted the story of Pharaoh, the sûrah now addresses the disbelievers who doubted the resurrection, challenging them to compare the creation of humanity with the creation of the sky. The question is rhetorical, for the answer is obvious: the sky, with its vastness and majesty, is far greater than the human being. Yet Allah built the sky, and so the One Who built the sky is certainly capable of recreating the human being, whose creation is comparatively trivial.\n\nThe verse establishes a principle that runs throughout the Qur'an: the resurrection is not difficult for Allah, for the One Who created the heavens and the earth in their immense grandeur can easily recreate the human being. The disbelievers' doubt about the resurrection stemmed from their failure to appreciate the power of Allah, who they saw at work every day in the vast creation of the sky yet failed to recognise. The sky itself, which they gazed upon constantly, was the evidence of the very power they doubted.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to reflect upon the greatness of creation as a proof of the power of Allah. The sky, the earth, and all that they contain are signs of the Creator's power, and they testify to His ability to bring the dead back to life. The believer is encouraged to contemplate the majesty of the universe, to recognise in it the hand of the All-Powerful, and to find in this recognition the certainty that the promise of the resurrection is true. The argument is simple yet profound: the One Who built the sky can certainly rebuild the human being.",
  "28": "This verse continues the description of the sky's creation: \"raising it high and forming it flawlessly.\" Having mentioned that Allah built the sky, the verse now describes the manner of its construction: He raised it high above the earth, and He formed it flawlessly, without any defect or imperfection. The vast height of the sky, the precision of its order, and the perfection of its design all testify to the power and wisdom of the Creator, who raised this immense structure and formed it with flawless precision.\n\nThe description of the sky as \"flawlessly\" formed (sawwāhā) is significant, for it points to the perfection of the heavens, in which the stars, the planets, and the celestial bodies move in precise and orderly orbits, without collision or deviation. The sky is not a random collection of matter but a carefully constructed edifice, formed with a precision that the human mind struggles to comprehend. This perfection is a sign of the One Who formed it, and it is a testament to the power that will recreate humanity on the Day of Resurrection.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to reflect upon the perfection of creation as a proof of the Creator's power. The flawless formation of the sky is evidence that the universe is the work of a Master Designer, and the believer is encouraged to contemplate this perfection and to recognise in it the certainty of the resurrection. If Allah formed the sky with such flawless precision, then the recreation of the human being is a simple matter for Him, and the promise of the Hereafter is as certain as the sky above.",
  "29": "This verse continues the description of the sky: \"He dimmed its night, and brought forth its daylight.\" Having described the raising and perfecting of the sky, the verse now describes the alternation of night and day that Allah established within it. He \"dimmed its night\" (aghṭasha), covering the sky in the darkness of night, and He \"brought forth its daylight\" (akhraja ḍuḥāhā), drawing out the light of day. The alternation of darkness and light is a sign of Allah's control over the sky He created, and it is a further testimony to His power.\n\nThe imagery of the verse is vivid and precise. The night is described as something that Allah \"dimmed\" — that is, He brought darkness over the sky, extinguishing the light of day. The daylight is described as something He \"brought forth\" — drawing it out from the darkness as a treasure is drawn from a hidden place. This language conveys the sense of deliberate, purposeful action, as though the night and day are brought forth by the direct command of the Creator, each in its appointed time and measure.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to reflect upon the alternation of night and day as a sign of Allah's power and wisdom. The rhythm of light and darkness, which governs all of life on earth, is not an accident but a deliberate design, established by the One Who created the sky and controls its affairs. The believer is encouraged to contemplate this rhythm, to recognise in it the hand of the Creator, and to find in it the certainty that the same power that brings forth the daylight from the night will bring forth the dead from their graves on the Day of Resurrection.",
  "30": "This verse extends the description to the earth: \"As for the earth, He spread it out as well.\" Having described the creation of the sky, the verse now turns to the earth, which Allah spread out after the sky, making it a level, habitable surface for His creation. The word daḥāhā, translated as \"spread it out,\" conveys the sense of expanding and levelling the earth, preparing it as a dwelling place for humanity and all living beings. The earth, with its mountains, valleys, plains, and seas, was formed by the power of Allah and made suitable for life.\n\nThe verse establishes the sequence of creation — the sky first, then the earth — and draws attention to the deliberate design of both. The earth was not left as a rough, uninhabitable mass but was spread out and prepared, its surface made level enough for people to walk upon, to build upon, and to cultivate. This preparation of the earth is a sign of Allah's care for His creation, for He did not merely create the earth but shaped it and made it habitable, providing for the needs of all who dwell upon it.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to reflect upon the earth as a sign of Allah's power and mercy. The ground beneath one's feet, which is so often taken for granted, is in fact a carefully prepared gift, spread out by the Creator for the benefit of His servants. The believer is encouraged to contemplate the earth, to recognise in its formation the hand of the All-Wise, and to respond with gratitude for the provision it offers. The verse also reinforces the argument for the resurrection, for the One Who spread out the earth can certainly gather humanity upon it for the final judgment.",
  "31": "This verse describes the further preparation of the earth: \"bringing forth its water and pastures.\" After spreading out the earth, Allah brought forth from it its water and its pastures — the springs, rivers, and groundwater that sustain life, and the vegetation and pastures that provide nourishment for animals and humans. The water and the pastures are the two fundamental provisions of the earth, and their emergence from the earth is a sign of the Creator's power and mercy.\n\nThe verse highlights the interconnectedness of the earth's provisions. The water, brought forth from the depths of the earth, gives rise to the pastures, the vegetation that covers the earth and feeds the animals that feed humanity. This chain of provision, from water to pasture to life, is a testament to the wisdom of the Creator, who designed the earth to sustain His creation in a continuous, interconnected system. Nothing is arbitrary; each provision leads to the next in a chain of mercy that extends to every living being.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to reflect upon the provisions of the earth as signs of Allah's mercy. The water that quenches thirst and the pastures that feed the animals are not mere natural resources but deliberate gifts from the Creator, brought forth by His power for the benefit of His creation. The believer is encouraged to contemplate these provisions, to recognise in them the hand of Allah, and to respond with gratitude and stewardship. The verse also points to the resurrection, for the One Who brings forth water and pastures from the dead earth can certainly bring forth the dead from their graves.",
  "32": "This verse completes the description of the earth's preparation: \"and setting the mountains firmly ˹upon it˺—\" After bringing forth the water and pastures, Allah set the mountains firmly upon the earth, anchoring them in place. The mountains, with their immense mass and their deep roots, serve to stabilise the earth, and their placement is described as an act of deliberate design. The em-dash at the end of the verse indicates that the thought continues into the next verse, connecting the setting of the mountains to the purpose they serve.\n\nThe setting of the mountains is a sign of Allah's power and wisdom. The mountains, which appear so permanent and immovable, are in fact placed by the command of Allah, their roots extending deep into the earth to hold it firm. They are not random formations but deliberate anchors, set in place by the Creator to stabilise the surface of the earth and to make it habitable. The verse thus presents the mountains as another element in the careful design of the earth, each part serving a purpose in the greater whole.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to reflect upon the mountains as signs of Allah's power. The peaks that rise above the earth, so solid and enduring, are themselves creations, set in place by the One Who has power over all things. The believer is encouraged to contemplate the mountains, to recognise in their firmness the power of the Creator, and to find in this recognition a deepening of faith and certainty. The verse also reinforces the theme of the sûrah, for the One Who set the mountains firmly can certainly raise the dead, and the permanence of the mountains is itself a reminder of the permanence of the Hereafter.",
  "33": "This verse completes the thought begun in the previous verse: \"all as ˹a means of˺ sustenance for you and your animals.\" The water, the pastures, and the firmly-set mountains — all of these were created by Allah as a provision for humanity and for the animals that serve them. The verse thus ties together the entire description of the earth's preparation, revealing its ultimate purpose: all of it was designed as sustenance, a provision from the Creator for the benefit of His creation. The earth is not an end in itself but a means, a gift prepared by Allah to sustain the life He has placed upon it.\n\nThe verse highlights the comprehensiveness of Allah's provision. The water quenches thirst, the pastures feed the animals, the mountains stabilise the earth and store the water, and all of it together sustains both humanity and the animals that share the earth with them. This provision is not limited to one group or one region but extends to all of creation, encompassing every living being in the web of sustenance that Allah has woven. The mention of \"your animals\" (anʿām) specifically draws attention to the livestock that serve humanity, whose provision is also arranged by Allah.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to recognise the earth and its resources as gifts from Allah, provided for the sustenance of His creation. The believer is encouraged to reflect upon this provision, to respond with gratitude, and to use the resources of the earth responsibly, as a trust from Allah. The verse also reinforces the argument for the resurrection, for the One Who provided the earth as sustenance for this life has certainly prepared a greater provision for the life to come, and the believer is urged to strive for that provision through faith and obedience.",
  "34": "This verse introduces the final theme of the sûrah: \"But, when the Supreme Disaster comes to pass—\" The verse turns from the signs of creation to the reality of the Hereafter, warning of \"the Supreme Disaster\" (aṭ-ṭāmmah al-kubrā), the overwhelming catastrophe that will engulf all of creation on the Day of Judgment. The word ṭāmmah conveys the sense of something that overwhelms and overcomes everything, and the description \"Supreme\" (kubrā) indicates that this disaster will be the greatest of all, surpassing every catastrophe that has ever occurred in this world.\n\nThe em-dash at the end of the verse indicates that the thought continues into the next verse, which will describe what this Supreme Disaster is. The verse thus creates a moment of suspense, drawing the listener's attention to the magnitude of what is coming before revealing its details. The Supreme Disaster is the Day of Judgment, the day when the world as we know it will come to an end and humanity will be brought to account, and the sûrah now turns to describe this day in vivid detail.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to live with the awareness of the Hereafter. The Supreme Disaster is not a distant myth but a certain reality, appointed by Allah and approaching with every passing moment. The believer is encouraged to reflect upon this reality, to prepare for it through faith and good deeds, and to keep it ever before their eyes as the ultimate horizon of their life. The verse serves as a powerful reminder that the pleasures and pursuits of this world are fleeting, and that the only thing that truly matters is one's preparation for the day when the Supreme Disaster will come to pass.",
  "35": "This verse describes the Supreme Disaster: \"the Day every person will remember all ˹their˺ striving.\" On that Day, when the Supreme Disaster comes to pass, every person will remember all that they strived for in this world — every deed, every effort, every ambition, and every pursuit. The word \"striving\" (saʿā) encompasses the totality of a person's efforts in life, both good and bad, and the verse declares that all of it will be brought to remembrance on that Day, when the veil of heedlessness is lifted and the reality of one's life is laid bare.\n\nThe word \"remember\" is significant, for it indicates that on that Day, every person will recall their striving with a clarity that was absent in this world. In this life, people are often heedless of the true nature of their deeds, distracted by the concerns of the moment and the illusions of the world. But on the Day of Judgment, this heedlessness will be lifted, and every person will remember exactly what they strove for and why, seeing their life in its true light. This remembrance is itself a form of reckoning, for it confronts the soul with the reality it had forgotten.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to live with the awareness that their striving will be remembered. Every effort, every choice, and every deed is being recorded, and the day will come when all of it will be recalled. The believer is encouraged to strive for that which will benefit them on that Day — the deeds of faith, righteousness, and devotion — and to avoid the striving that leads to regret. The verse is a call to make one's striving in this world a striving for the Hereafter, so that the remembrance of it on that Day may be a source of joy rather than sorrow.",
  "36": "This verse continues the description of the Day: \"and the Hellfire will be displayed for all to see—\" On the Day of Judgment, the Hellfire will be brought forth and displayed openly, visible to all who are gathered. The word \"displayed\" (burrizat) conveys the sense of being brought out into the open, exposed and revealed for all to see. The Hellfire, which had been hidden from human sight during this world, will be made visible on that Day, and its terrifying reality will be undeniable to all who witness it.\n\nThe display of the Hellfire serves multiple purposes. For the disbelievers, it is a source of terror, for they will see with their own eyes the punishment they had denied and mocked, and the reality of their fate will be made manifest before them. For the believers, it is a source of gratitude and reassurance, for they will see the punishment from which Allah has saved them, and their hearts will overflow with thankfulness for His mercy. The display of the Hellfire is thus a moment of profound reckoning for all of humanity, a visual confirmation of the reality of the Hereafter.\n\nThe em-dash at the end of the verse indicates that the thought continues into the next verse. The verse teaches the believer to take the reality of the Hellfire seriously, for it is not a metaphor or a myth but a real punishment that will be displayed openly on the Day of Judgment. The believer is encouraged to seek Allah's protection from the Fire, to live in a way that keeps them far from it, and to be grateful for the guidance that shows the way to safety. The verse is a reminder that the Hereafter is not hidden forever but will one day be revealed for all to see.",
  "37": "This verse begins the division of humanity on the Day of Judgment: \"then as for those who transgressed,\" After the display of the Hellfire, the fate of each person will be determined according to their deeds, and the verse begins with those who transgressed — the ones who exceeded all bounds, rebelled against Allah, and followed their desires without restraint. The word ṭaghā conveys the sense of overflowing beyond limits, of arrogance so complete that it acknowledges no boundary and no authority above itself.\n\nThe transgression described here is the opposite of the fear of Allah described earlier in the sûrah. The one who fears Allah lives within the limits He has set, while the one who transgresses breaks through every limit, following their own desires as their guide and their own will as their law. This transgression takes many forms — arrogance, oppression, denial of the truth, and immersion in sin — but its essence is always the same: the refusal to submit to the authority of Allah and the insistence on living according to one's own whims.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer about the reality of transgression and its consequences. The transgression described here is not a minor failing but a fundamental orientation of the heart, a choice to live without regard for the limits of Allah. The believer is called to guard against this orientation, to live within the boundaries that Allah has set, and to cultivate the fear of Allah that keeps the soul from overflowing into transgression. The verse sets the stage for the division that follows, contrasting the fate of the transgressors with the fate of those who feared their Lord.",
  "38": "This verse continues the description of the transgressors: \"and preferred the ˹fleeting˺ life of this world,\" The transgressors are further described as those who preferred the life of this world — the fleeting, temporary life of comfort and pleasure — over the eternal life of the Hereafter. Their transgression was rooted in this preference, for they chose the immediate gratification of this world over the lasting reward of the next, trading the eternal for the temporary and the real for the illusory.\n\nThe description of the worldly life as \"fleeting\" is significant, for it captures the essential nature of this world: it passes away quickly, and its pleasures are temporary and ultimately unsatisfying. The word translated as \"fleeting\" (dunyā) is itself related to the idea of nearness and lowness, indicating that this world is the lower, lesser life compared to the Hereafter. The one who prefers this fleeting life has made a catastrophic choice, exchanging that which endures for that which perishes.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer about the importance of priorities. The choice between this world and the Hereafter is the fundamental choice of every life, and the believer is called to prefer the Hereafter, to live in this world as a traveller passing through, and to invest in that which endures rather than that which fades. The verse does not condemn the lawful enjoyment of this world but condemns the preference for it over the Hereafter, the choice to make this fleeting life the ultimate goal. The believer is encouraged to keep their priorities clear, to seek the Hereafter first, and to use this world only as a means to that end.",
  "39": "This verse declares the fate of the transgressors: \"the Hellfire will certainly be ˹their˺ home.\" For those who transgressed and preferred the fleeting life of this world, the Hellfire will be their home — their final destination and their eternal dwelling. The word \"home\" (ma'wā) is deeply ironic, for a home is normally a place of comfort, safety, and belonging, yet for the transgressors, their \"home\" will be the blazing Fire, a place of torment and despair from which there is no escape.\n\nThe certainty of this declaration — \"will certainly be\" — leaves no room for doubt. The fate of the transgressors is not a matter of speculation but of divine decree, determined by their own choices in this world. The Hellfire is not an arbitrary punishment but the natural consequence of a life lived in transgression and preference for the fleeting world, and it is described as their \"home\" because they will dwell in it forever, with no possibility of leaving. The word \"home\" also conveys the sense of belonging, as though the transgressors, by their own choice, have made themselves fit only for this dwelling.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer about the finality of the Hereafter. The choice made in this world determines the home of the next, and there is no change or reversal after death. The believer is urged to consider carefully which home they are preparing for themselves, and to live in a way that makes their eternal home the Paradise of their Lord rather than the Fire. The verse is a sobering warning, but it is also an invitation, for the door of repentance remains open, and the one who turns back from transgression can still change their final destination.",
  "40": "This verse presents the contrasting fate of the righteous: \"And as for those who were in awe of standing before their Lord and restrained themselves from ˹evil˺ desires,\" The verse describes the second group — those who feared the moment of standing before their Lord for judgment, and who, as a result of this fear, restrained themselves from following their evil desires. Their fear of Allah was not a vague sentiment but a practical force in their lives, holding them back from sin and guiding them towards righteousness.\n\nThe verse identifies two defining qualities of the righteous. The first is \"awe of standing before their Lord\" — the awareness that they will one day stand before Allah for judgment, and the fear that this awareness produces. The second is \"restraining themselves from evil desires\" — the discipline of the soul, by which they held back their desires from what Allah has forbidden, refusing to let their whims lead them into transgression. These two qualities — the fear of Allah and the control of desire — are the foundation of the righteous life, and they are the opposite of the transgression described in the preceding verses.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer about the essence of righteousness. The righteous person is not one who has no desires, but one who controls their desires through the fear of Allah, restraining the soul from that which displeases Him and directing it towards that which pleases Him. The believer is called to cultivate this fear of Allah and this discipline of the soul, to live with the awareness of the Day of Judgment, and to hold back their desires from every forbidden path. The verse sets up the conclusion that follows, in which the reward of these righteous ones will be declared, contrasting their fate with that of the transgressors.",
  "41": "The description of the righteous in the previous verse—those who stood in awe of their Lord and restrained themselves from evil desires—now reaches its beautiful culmination: “Paradise will certainly be their home.” The Arabic particle inna brings emphatic certainty, and the sentence structure “Paradise, it is the home” leaves no room for doubt.\n\nThe word al-Jannah means a garden, a place of lush, hidden beauty, and it is the same word used in Surah An-Naba’ for the dense orchards that serve as a glimpse of the eternal gardens to come. Here, it is the full, unblemished reality.\n\nThe term ma’wā, translated as “home,” carries the sense of a refuge, a dwelling place, a place of return and ultimate belonging. This is the same word that was used for the Hellfire being the home of the transgressors in verse 39, and that parallel is deliberate. The righteous and the wicked each have their ma’wā, their final, fitting abode. For the righteous, the home is not a place of torment but of perfect peace, joy and honour. In this world, the believers were often strangers, misunderstood and persecuted for their faith. The Prophet ﷺ said, “Islam began as something strange, and it will return to being strange, so glad tidings to the strangers.” Paradise is the true homeland for those strangers. It is the restoration of the garden that Adam and Hawwa’ were created for and then temporarily left. The righteous, through their awe of the standing before Allah and their restraint over their desires, have earned this return. The verse is a promise that the fear they felt at the thought of standing before their Lord is now replaced by eternal security. Their home is one where there is no idle talk, no lying, no fear, no death. The angels will greet them with peace, and they will dwell in what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and no human heart has imagined. The certainty of “will certainly be” comforts every believer who struggles against the pulls of the lower self, reassuring them that the struggle is temporary and the reward is everlasting. The surah has moved from the violent oaths of the angels extracting souls, to the cosmic quaking, to the example of Pharaoh’s destruction, to the rational proof of creation, and now to the final, peaceful destination of those who answer the call. Paradise as their home is the fitting reward from the Most Compassionate, the generous gift mentioned in the earlier surah. It is the ultimate success, the mafāz, the salvation that every believing heart longs for.",
  "42": "After the grand dichotomy of the two destinies, the surah now addresses one of the persistent questions that the deniers used to pose to the Prophet ﷺ: “They ask you, O Prophet, regarding the Hour, ‘When will it be?’” The “they” refers to the pagan Quraysh, though the question has been asked by sceptics in every age. Their asking was not a sincere quest for guidance but a mixture of mockery, a challenge, and an attempt to trip up the Prophet. They thought that if he could not specify the exact timing of the Day of Judgement, his entire message was discredited.\n\nThe question “When will it be?” uses the Arabic word ayyāna, which seeks a precise point in time. It reveals their materialist mindset: they wanted the Hour to be scheduled, like a market day or a seasonal fair. The deep irony is that the very question they asked mockingly is the question that every human being should ask sincerely, with a heart full of concern and a desire to prepare. The Qur’an records their question to expose their arrogance and to provide the proper divine response. The Hour, as-sā‘ah, is one of the names of the Day of Resurrection, emphasising its suddenness and its inevitability. It is the moment when time as we know it ends and eternity begins. The Prophet ﷺ himself did not know when the Hour would occur. In the famous hadith of Jibrīl, when the angel asked, “When is the Hour?” the Prophet replied, “The one questioned knows no more than the questioner.” The knowledge of the Hour was kept hidden by divine wisdom, for if humans knew the exact date, they might either be paralysed by fear or emboldened to delay repentance until the last moment. The concealment serves to keep every generation in a state of readiness. The verse transitions the surah from the vivid depictions of the Supreme Disaster to the practical reality that its timing is a secret of the unseen. The deniers’ question is about to receive a firm and clarifying answer that reorients the Prophet’s mission and the believers’ focus from idle speculation to urgent action.",
  "43": "The divine response to their question is direct and absolving: “But it is not for you to tell its time.” The Arabic phrase fīma anta min dhikrāhā is a powerful construction that literally means “In what capacity are you to mention it?” It is a rhetorical question that negates any role for the Prophet in determining or disclosing the Hour’s timing. The Prophet is the messenger, the warner, the bearer of glad tidings, but he is not the keeper of the unseen keys. Allah is telling him that this matter is entirely outside his jurisdiction. This verse served to relieve the Prophet from the pressure the Quraysh tried to put on him. They wanted him to prove his prophethood by predicting the exact date, but his prophethood was never about fortune-telling; it was about conveying the message of monotheism, morality and accountability. The knowledge of the Hour is part of ‘ilm al-ghayb, the knowledge of the unseen, which belongs exclusively to Allah.\n\nThe verse also serves as an important doctrinal clarification. Some might mistakenly think that the Prophet ﷺ, due to his elevated status, had access to all unseen matters, but the Qur’an consistently teaches that even he did not know the Hour. In Surah Al-A‘raf, the Prophet is instructed to say, “I do not know whether what you are promised is near or whether my Lord will extend it for a period” (7:187). This protects the belief from exaggeration about the Prophet and keeps the focus on Allah alone as the Knower of the unseen.\n\nThe phrase “to tell its time” translates dhikrāhā, which can also mean to make mention of it, to discuss its timing with authority. The Prophet’s role is to warn about its reality, not to pinpoint its occurrence.\n\nThe answer, therefore, shifts the question from the “when” to the “what now.” The deniers are fixated on a detail that is none of their business; what should concern them is their state when it arrives. The verse is a masterclass in redirecting a futile question to a beneficial truth.",
  "44": "The negation of the Prophet’s knowledge is now completed with an affirmation of where the true knowledge resides: “That knowledge rests with your Lord alone.” The word muntahāhā comes from intahā, meaning its ultimate end, its final destination, its utmost limit. The knowledge of the Hour is not scattered or partially known by various beings; it is solely and entirely with Allah, terminating at Him. No angel, no prophet, no created being shares in this knowledge. The verse uses the intimate phrase “your Lord” (rabbika), addressing the Prophet directly, which brings comfort to him and, by extension, to every believer. The Lord who nurtures, guides and protects you is the One who holds the timing of the Hour in His wisdom. The exclusivity of this knowledge is emphasised elsewhere, such as in Surah Luqman: “Indeed, Allah has knowledge of the Hour, and He sends down the rain, and He knows what is in the wombs” (31:34). These are the five keys of the unseen that no one else knows. The verse thus re-establishes the correct hierarchy of knowledge: the Creator knows, the creation does not, and the creation must trust the Creator’s wisdom in keeping it hidden.\n\nFor the disbelievers, this is a warning: since you do not know when it will come, you cannot afford to delay repentance. For the believers, it is a motivation to live each day as if it could be the last.\n\nThe phrase “rests with your Lord” also implies that the matter is settled, complete and perfectly managed. There is no anxiety for the Prophet in not knowing; his Lord has it under control. The surah is now ready to redefine the Prophet’s role in light of this hidden knowledge.\n\nThe question of the Hour is not a puzzle to be solved but a reality to be prepared for, and the preparation comes through the warning that the Prophet is commanded to deliver.",
  "45": "Having removed the burden of knowing the Hour, the verse clarifies the Prophet’s actual mission: “Your duty is only to warn whoever is in awe of it.” The word innamā (only) restricts and focuses the Prophet’s role. He is a mundhir, a warner. His task is not to answer every speculative question or to force people to believe, but to deliver the warning clearly and to let the warning do its work in hearts that are receptive. The warning is effective only for “whoever is in awe of it” (man yakhshāhā).\n\nThis is a profound insight. The same message that softens the heart of one person hardens the heart of another. The deciding factor is khashyah: a pre-existing awe, a fear, a reverence for the truth that resides in the person’s soul. Those who already have that seed of awe will hear the warning and take it to heart, increasing in fear of Allah and in righteous action. Those whose hearts are dead, filled with arrogance and love of the world, will hear the same warning and mock it, as the Quraysh did. The verse therefore liberates the Prophet from feeling responsible for the disbelief of the mockers. He cannot guide those who have shut their hearts to awe. His duty is discharged when he warns; the outcome is with Allah. This also connects back to verse 40, which described the righteous as those who “were in awe of standing before their Lord.” The Prophet’s warning is precisely for such people, strengthening their awe and guiding them further. For the deniers who lack this awe, the warning only adds to their evidence against themselves on the Day of Judgement. The verse is a lesson for all callers to Islam: the success of the call is not measured by the number of converts but by the sincerity and clarity with which the message is delivered. The focus should be on nurturing those hearts that tremble at the mention of the Hour, not on chasing the approval of the scoffers. The surah thus ends its main discourse by re-centring the mission on the quality of the audience’s heart, setting up the final, haunting image of the Day when the mockers will realise their error.",
  "46": "The surah concludes with a striking image that answers the deniers’ obsession with time by showing them how they will perceive time when the Hour arrives: “On the Day they see it, it will be as if they had stayed in the world no more than one evening or its morning.” The deniers, who spent their lives arguing about the timing of the Hour and thinking it far off, will be confronted with a reality that shrinks their entire earthly existence into a fleeting moment.\n\nThe word “see it” means they will witness the Hour with their own eyes, and at that moment, their perception of the past will be completely transformed. The long decades they lived, the plans they made, the pleasures they enjoyed, the power they wielded—all of it will seem like a brief stay, no longer than a single evening (‘ashiyyah) or the morning that follows it (ḍuḥāhā). The alternation of evening and morning echoes the earlier mention of Allah bringing forth the daylight after dimming the night. The entire span of a human life will seem as short as that daily transition.\n\nThis is not a literal statement about time shrinking, but a psychological and existential truth: in the face of eternity, even a hundred years is an insignificant speck. The disbelievers’ earlier mockery about “when will it be?” will be answered not with a calendar date but with an experience that renders the question meaningless.\n\nThe verse also carries a deep sense of tragedy and regret. The same life that they thought was so long and significant, in which they delayed repentance and mocked the warners, is now seen as a wasted opportunity, a mere moment that could have been used for eternal joy but was squandered.\n\nThe phrase “one evening or its morning” specifically uses the parts of the day that are transitional, underscoring the impermanence of worldly life. The morning is followed by the full heat of day, just as the worldly life is followed by the full reality of the Hereafter.\n\nThe surah ends on this sobering note, leaving the listener with the urgent realisation that the time to prepare is now, not later. The Hour may be hidden, but its sudden arrival will make every moment of heedlessness a source of unbearable remorse for those who did not take the path of awe and purification. With this, the message of the surah is complete: the resurrection is inevitable, the destinies are fixed, and the wise are those who live in the light of that certainty."
};
